---
// The piece. One page, scenes as sections. See docs/10-sprint-02.md.
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');
---

<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <meta charset="utf-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1, viewport-fit=cover" />
    <meta name="color-scheme" content="light dark" />
    <title>A shape that never repeats</title>
    <meta
      name="description"
      content="One ordinary-looking shape can cover an infinite floor, and the pattern can never repeat. Here is why it can't."
    />
    <link rel="canonical" href={`https://oddurs.github.io${base}/`} />
  </head>

  <body>
    <article>
      <header class="lede">
        <p class="eyebrow">The einstein tile</p>
        <h1>A shape that never repeats</h1>
        <p class="standfirst">
          In 2022 a retired print technician, cutting shapes out of card at his
          kitchen table, found something mathematicians had hunted for fifty
          years: a single tile that covers an endless floor without the pattern
          ever repeating. Here is what that means, and why it can’t.
        </p>
      </header>

      <!-- Scene 2 — meet the hat -->
      <section class="scene" data-scene="hat" aria-labelledby="h-hat">
        <div class="prose">
          <h2 id="h-hat">The shape itself</h2>
          <p>
            Before any of that: this is not an exotic object. Take a floor of
            hexagons, cut each one into six kites, and glue eight of the kites
            together.
          </p>
        </div>

        <figure class="stage">
          <canvas data-canvas aria-describedby="alt-hat"></canvas>
          <figcaption class="hud">
            <p data-caption class="hud-prompt"></p>
            <div class="hud-row slider-row">
              <label for="step-2" class="visually-hidden">construction step</label>
              <input id="step-2" data-step type="range" />
              <span data-readout class="meter"></span>
            </div>
          </figcaption>
        </figure>

        <p id="alt-hat" class="takeaway">
          The hat is eight kites cut from a hexagon floor — a thirteen-sided
          polygon with no unusual angles. Shapes like it had been catalogued and
          overlooked before, because nobody had tried to tile with one.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- Scene 3 — try to make it repeat -->
      <section class="scene" data-scene="repeat" aria-labelledby="h-repeat">
        <div class="prose">
          <h2 id="h-repeat">What “repeats” actually means</h2>
          <p>
            A pattern repeats when you can slide a copy of it onto itself and
            have everything land. That is not a figure of speech — it is the
            whole definition. Try it on a hexagon floor, then try it on this.
          </p>
        </div>

        <figure class="stage">
          <canvas data-canvas aria-describedby="alt-repeat"></canvas>
          <figcaption class="hud">
            <h3 data-title class="hud-title"></h3>
            <p data-prompt class="hud-prompt"></p>
            <div class="gauge">
              <div class="track"><div data-bar class="bar"></div></div>
              <span data-meter class="meter"></span>
            </div>
            <div class="hud-row">
              <button data-next type="button" hidden></button>
              <button data-reset type="button">Show me the best there is</button>
            </div>
          </figcaption>
        </figure>

        <p id="alt-repeat" class="takeaway">
          A hexagon floor slides onto itself perfectly. The einstein tiling never
          does — the closest any slide gets is 46% of tiles landing.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- Scene 4 — order is not randomness -->
      <section class="scene" data-scene="recurrence" aria-labelledby="h-recur">
        <div class="prose">
          <h2 id="h-recur">But it isn’t random</h2>
          <p>
            “Never repeats” sounds like chaos, and it is easy to conclude the
            tiling is just a mess. It is the opposite. Point at any patch of it.
          </p>
        </div>

        <figure class="stage">
          <canvas data-canvas aria-describedby="alt-recur"></canvas>
          <figcaption class="hud">
            <p data-prompt class="hud-prompt"></p>
            <p class="hint">
              Tap the tiling to pick a patch.
              <span data-readout class="meter"></span>
            </p>
          </figcaption>
        </figure>

        <p id="alt-recur" class="takeaway">
          Any patch you can point at occurs again elsewhere — typically dozens of
          times within view, and infinitely often on an endless floor. The tiling
          is highly ordered. What it lacks is a single slide that carries the
          whole of it onto itself.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- Scene 5 — the hierarchy -->
      <section class="scene" data-scene="hierarchy" aria-labelledby="h-hierarchy">
        <div class="prose">
          <h2 id="h-hierarchy">Why it can’t</h2>
          <p>
            Zoom out, and the tiles turn out to be grouped — and the groups are
            grouped, and those groups are grouped, all the way up.
          </p>
        </div>

        <figure class="stage">
          <canvas data-canvas aria-describedby="alt-hierarchy"></canvas>
          <figcaption class="hud">
            <p data-caption class="hud-prompt"></p>
            <div class="hud-row slider-row">
              <span aria-hidden="true" class="end">tiles</span>
              <label for="stage-5" class="visually-hidden">grouping level</label>
              <input id="stage-5" data-stage type="range" />
              <span aria-hidden="true" class="end">whole</span>
            </div>
            <p class="hint">
              Pinch or scroll to zoom — the grouping follows.
              <span data-readout class="meter"></span>
            </p>
          </figcaption>
        </figure>

        <p id="alt-hierarchy" class="takeaway">
          Tiles group into four kinds of cluster; those clusters group into the
          same four kinds, larger; and so on without end. A repeating pattern
          needs a block you can stamp out. This has no such block at any size —
          which is exactly why it can never repeat.
        </p>
      </section>

      <footer class="outro">
        <p>
          The shape is called <strong>the hat</strong>. David Smith found it in
          November 2022; he worked out what it meant with Joseph Samuel Myers,
          Craig S. Kaplan and Chaim Goodman-Strauss, who published the proof in
          March 2023. It had been catalogued before, and overlooked, more than
          once.
        </p>
        <p class="fine">
          <span>Built from the authors’ own substitution data.</span>{' '}
          <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2303.10798">Read the paper</a>{' · '}
          <a href="https://github.com/oddurs/einstein-tile">source</a>
        </p>
      </footer>
    </article>

    <script>
      import { mountScenes } from '../scenes/stage.js';
      import { mountHatScene } from '../scenes/thehat.js';
      import { mountRepeatScene } from '../scenes/repeat.js';
      import { mountRecurrenceScene } from '../scenes/recurrence.js';
      import { mountHierarchyScene } from '../scenes/hierarchy.js';

      mountScenes({
        hat: mountHatScene,
        repeat: mountRepeatScene,
        recurrence: mountRecurrenceScene,
        hierarchy: mountHierarchyScene,
      });
    </script>

    <style is:global>
      /* Surfaces match palette.ts SURFACE — every contrast and CVD number in
         the palette was computed against exactly these. */
      :root {
        --bg: #fcfcfb;
        --fg: #1a1a19;
        --muted: color-mix(in oklab, #1a1a19 62%, #fcfcfb);
        --panel: rgba(252, 252, 251, 0.92);
        --line: rgba(26, 26, 25, 0.14);
        --accent: #3eb4f1;
        --good: #1f9d55;
        --measure: 34rem;
      }
      @media (prefers-color-scheme: dark) {
        :root {
          --bg: #1a1a19;
          --fg: #f0efec;
          --muted: color-mix(in oklab, #f0efec 66%, #1a1a19);
          --panel: rgba(26, 26, 25, 0.92);
          --line: rgba(240, 239, 236, 0.16);
          --accent: #0e97d2;
          --good: #35b26a;
        }
      }

      * { box-sizing: border-box; }

      html { scroll-behavior: smooth; }
      @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
        html { scroll-behavior: auto; }
        * { transition: none !important; animation: none !important; }
      }

      body {
        margin: 0;
        background: var(--bg);
        color: var(--fg);
        font: 400 clamp(17px, 1.1vw + 15px, 19px)/1.6 ui-serif, Georgia,
          'Times New Roman', serif;
        -webkit-text-size-adjust: 100%;
      }

      article {
        max-width: 62rem;
        margin: 0 auto;
        padding: 0 max(20px, env(safe-area-inset-left)) 8rem;
      }

      .prose, .lede, .outro, .takeaway {
        max-width: var(--measure);
        margin-inline: auto;
      }

      .lede { padding: 22vh 0 12vh; }

      .eyebrow {
        margin: 0 0 1.2rem;
        font-family: ui-sans-serif, system-ui, sans-serif;
        font-size: 13px;
        font-weight: 500;
        letter-spacing: 0.09em;
        text-transform: uppercase;
        color: var(--muted);
      }

      h1 {
        margin: 0 0 1.4rem;
        font-size: clamp(2.4rem, 7vw, 4rem);
        font-weight: 400;
        line-height: 1.05;
        letter-spacing: -0.02em;
        text-wrap: balance;
      }

      .standfirst {
        margin: 0;
        font-size: 1.12em;
        line-height: 1.55;
        color: var(--muted);
      }

      h2 {
        margin: 0 0 0.8rem;
        font-size: clamp(1.5rem, 3.6vw, 2rem);
        font-weight: 400;
        line-height: 1.15;
        letter-spacing: -0.01em;
        text-wrap: balance;
      }

      p { margin: 0 0 1.1rem; }

      .scene { margin: 0 0 14vh; }

      /* The stage is deliberately NOT full-height. The canvas takes
         `touch-action: none` so it can own drag and pinch, which means a
         full-bleed canvas would swallow vertical scrolling and strand a reader
         on a phone. Keeping it under two-thirds of the viewport guarantees
         there is always ordinary page above and below to scroll with. */
      .stage {
        position: relative;
        margin: 2rem 0;
        border: 1px solid var(--line);
        border-radius: 16px;
        overflow: hidden;
        background: var(--bg);
      }

      .stage canvas {
        display: block;
        width: 100%;
        height: 62dvh;
        max-height: 640px;
        touch-action: none;
      }

      .hud {
        padding: 16px 18px;
        border-top: 1px solid var(--line);
        background: var(--panel);
      }

      .hud-title {
        margin: 0 0 6px;
        font-family: ui-sans-serif, system-ui, sans-serif;
        font-size: 12px;
        font-weight: 500;
        letter-spacing: 0.06em;
        text-transform: uppercase;
        color: var(--muted);
      }

      .hud-prompt {
        margin: 0 0 12px;
        font-size: 1em;
        line-height: 1.4;
        text-wrap: balance;
        min-height: 2.8em;
      }

      .hud-row { display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 10px; }
      .slider-row { gap: 12px; }

      .gauge {
        display: flex;
        align-items: center;
        gap: 12px;
        margin-bottom: 12px;
      }

      .track {
        flex: 1;
        height: 8px;
        border-radius: 999px;
        background: color-mix(in oklab, var(--fg) 12%, transparent);
        overflow: hidden;
      }
      .bar {
        height: 100%;
        width: 0;
        border-radius: 999px;
        background: var(--accent);
        transition: width 90ms linear;
      }
      .bar[data-perfect='true'] { background: var(--good); }

      .meter {
        font-family: ui-sans-serif, system-ui, sans-serif;
        font-size: 12.5px;
        font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
        color: var(--muted);
        white-space: nowrap;
      }

      .hint {
        display: flex;
        gap: 10px;
        margin: 10px 0 0;
        font-family: ui-sans-serif, system-ui, sans-serif;
        font-size: 12.5px;
        color: var(--muted);
      }
      .hint .meter { margin-left: auto; }

      .end {
        font-family: ui-sans-serif, system-ui, sans-serif;
        font-size: 12px;
        text-transform: lowercase;
        color: var(--muted);
      }

      input[type='range'] { flex: 1; min-width: 0; min-height: 44px; }

      button {
        min-height: 44px;
        padding: 0 16px;
        border: 1px solid var(--line);
        border-radius: 9px;
        background: transparent;
        color: inherit;
        font-family: ui-sans-serif, system-ui, sans-serif;
        font-size: 14px;
      }
      button[data-next]:not([hidden]) {
        border-color: transparent;
        background: var(--fg);
        color: var(--bg);
      }

      :focus-visible {
        outline: 2px solid var(--accent);
        outline-offset: 2px;
        border-radius: 4px;
      }

      /* The takeaway is the scene's meaning in words. It carries the point for
         anyone who cannot use the canvas, and for anyone who skims. */
      .takeaway {
        margin: 0;
        padding-left: 1rem;
        border-left: 2px solid var(--line);
        font-size: 0.95em;
        color: var(--muted);
      }

      .outro { padding-top: 2rem; }
      .fine { font-size: 0.85em; color: var(--muted); }
      a { color: inherit; text-underline-offset: 3px; }

      .visually-hidden {
        position: absolute;
        width: 1px;
        height: 1px;
        margin: -1px;
        overflow: hidden;
        clip-path: inset(50%);
        white-space: nowrap;
      }

      [data-failed='true'] .stage { display: none; }
    </style>
  </body>
</html>
